# The window needs GTK 4 and libadwaita through GObject introspection, and
# `gearbench-ctl gui` says what is missing and exits 1 rather than failing to
# import. The daemon uses python3-gi's Gio for D-Bus: its exit at a login
# screen, following the screen lock, blank and a suspend (the lights off, a
# running macro stopped and held keys let go), holding bindings and macros
# back while another account or a text console has the seat, a synced mouse's
# idle dimming, every notice, the tray icon and the focused window from
# Gearbench's GNOME Shell extension. Without it those are off, the journal
# says python3-gi is missing when the service starts, and the rest works. So
# these are Recommends: apt installs them by default and a headless box can
# leave them out.
Recommends: python3-gi,
            gir1.2-gtk-4.0,
            gir1.2-adw-1
Description: control for Logitech G keyboards and mice, the G432 and the Astro A50 Gen 4
 Gearbench drives Logitech G keyboards and mice over their vendor HID++ 2.0
 interfaces, on a cable or through a LIGHTSPEED receiver, and probes what
 each one can do from its own feature table. The README's table lists the
 models. Those not yet checked on the hardware are trials: the G515
 LIGHTSPEED TKL, G915 TKL and G815 keyboards and the G502 X, G502 X
 LIGHTSPEED, G502 X PLUS MILLENNIUM FALCON, G502 LIGHTSPEED, PRO X
 SUPERLIGHT, PRO WIRELESS and G903 LIGHTSPEED HERO mice. It remaps the G
 keys and the MR shift layer, sets per-key and zone lighting, records and
 replays macros through a uinput virtual keyboard, binds the extra buttons
 of each mouse but the G305, and sets each mouse's DPI and report rate and,
 on the G502 X PLUS, G305 and G502 HERO, its DPI stages.
 .
 It also drives the Astro A50 Gen 4 base station over its own vendor HID
 interface, which is not HID++: microphone level, sidetone, noise gate and
 mic EQ, the three EQ presets (Astro Command Center's .astroeq files too),
 the stream port mix and the base station's own settings, and it reads the
 headset's battery. It lists the A50's Game and Chat as two outputs.
 .
 For the Logitech G432 it sets the sidetone, and adds an equalizer output
 and noise suppression for its microphone. Gearbench Spatial, a 7.1 output
 for games rendered for headphones with SADIE II's measured heads, plays to
 either headset and is off until switched on.
 .
 A user systemd service owns the devices and holds the keyboard's software
 lighting claim; the command line and the GTK 4 window are front ends that talk
 to it over a unix socket. Nothing runs as root. Device access comes from
 udev rule files that tag nodes with uaccess, which grants them to the user
 of the active seat: the control interface of each keyboard, on its cable
 or its receiver, of each cabled mouse, of the A50's base station and of
 the G432's adapter, and the node a receiver gives a device paired to it.
 A mouse's node carries its motion and buttons too and, going by the
 kernel's source, would carry the keystrokes of a keyboard paired to the
 same receiver after the mouse, so pair nothing else there. The G815's
 control interface and the nodes of the G515 LIGHTSPEED TKL and G915 TKL
 behind their receivers carry what is typed on them, and the G502 HERO's
 and G502 X's control interfaces the keys their buttons are bound to type,
 so with those rules any program that user runs can read them. A program
 that opened such a node keeps reading it after another account takes the
 seat, and so reads what that account types. Another file tags /dev/uinput
 too, the line steam-devices ships. With it any program that user runs can
 create a virtual input device. The G502 X PLUS's own event nodes are the
 gearbench group's to read, which the package creates; with host mode off,
 the extra buttons need the user's account in it.
 .
 The keyboard's onboard profile is never written. Five mouse commands do write
 a mouse's onboard profile 1, each backing up every profile sector first and
 reading the sector back after. The A50's settings reach its base station's
 flash only when saved.
